1
00:00:00,183 --> 00:00:12,183
ODIN Pocket: a language model built from its first weight.
GIBC V2 — Track 01: Foundational LLM Development.

2
00:00:12,205 --> 00:00:24,205
49,295,872 trainable parameters, including embeddings and output.
The whole model fits below the 50 million parameter limit.

3
00:00:25,611 --> 00:00:39,611
This continuation is generated by our local checkpoint on CPU.
There is no inference API, retrieval substitute, or scripted answer.

4
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:51,650
It is a base language model: give it a beginning, not an instruction.
Its generated text can be repetitive or factually wrong.

5
00:00:51,723 --> 00:01:03,723
The tokens below come from that exact generation.
Select one to inspect the probability before sampling.

6
00:01:03,862 --> 00:01:17,862
The second tool compares possible endings using causal log likelihood.
Both candidates are scored by the same model.

7
00:01:17,887 --> 00:01:29,887
We normalize by candidate token count. Higher means more likely,
not more truthful: this preference is not a fact check.

8
00:01:30,007 --> 00:01:44,007
Training hardware: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070.
983,040,000 tokens processed in 3.92 recorded hours.

9
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:56,013
The development loss curve comes directly from the training log.
The page shows only evidence tied to the loaded training run.

10
00:01:56,049 --> 00:02:10,049
These are full, zero-shot evaluations through lm-evaluation-harness:
HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, PIQA and WinoGrande.

11
00:02:10,052 --> 00:02:22,052
WikiText-103 measures held-out language modeling.
Token perplexity depends on the tokenizer; the full protocol is documented.

12
00:02:22,136 --> 00:02:34,136
Weights start randomly. Training uses public FineWeb-Edu and WikiText text.
No pretrained weights, private teacher targets, or distillation.

13
00:02:34,138 --> 00:02:46,138
Checkpoint identity, source code, training recipe and measured results
make the experiment reproducible. Development was assisted by OpenAI Codex.
